(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
 "Gaussian boson sampling (GBS) replaces the single photons of boson sampling \
with squeezed light, and the permanent with the hafnian. It is the model \
behind the quantum-advantage experiments Jiuzhang and Borealis. With \
squeezers as network elements, the ",
 StyleBox["quantum interferometer",
  FontWeight->"Plain"],
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontWeight->"Plain",
  FontSlant->"Plain"],
 StyleBox["object",
  FontWeight->"Plain"],
 StyleBox["  ",
  FontWeight->"Plain",
  FontSlant->"Plain"],
 "computes exact GBS amplitudes, probabilities and samples. We build GBS \
networks from squeezers and beam splitters, check them against the hafnian \
formula, and show that any pure Gaussian state is a column of squeezers \
followed by one interferometer. Heralded two-mode squeezed vacua recover \
standard boson sampling. We then test a simulated device the way the \
experiments do.  In a twelve-mode Jiuzhang in miniature, the torontonian \
predicts the clicks of threshold detectors, and loss breaks the photon pairs. \
Finally, encoding a graph in the squeezers makes GBS sample dense subgraphs. \
We also emphasize  with how the cost of a hafnian grows with the photon \
number to indicate a classical computer is not suitable for this problem in a \
larger scale."
}], "Abstract",
 CellFrame->{{0, 0}, {0, 0.5}},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0.75],
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0026238532286587`*^9, 4.0026239026415405`*^9}, {
  4.002623961685646*^9, 
  4.0026241095219593`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2d74639e-5844-f248-9ead-\
b8938895fe78"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Introduction", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.002125731347233*^9, 
  4.0021257334218063`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f83e4764-7ff4-8740-afe2-\
0a7eab066029"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A linear interferometer on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"m", " "}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f717a5f6-d233-8741-bdd4-cd631742e21e"],
 "modes is a unitary transfer matrix ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["W", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7debac70-1b71-3b42-b88f-fc0738bb79cb"],
 ", with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"U", " ", 
     SubsuperscriptBox["a", "k", "\[Dagger]"], 
     SuperscriptBox["U", "\[Dagger]"]}], "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
      RowBox[{" ", "j", " "}]], 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["W", 
       RowBox[{"j", " ", "k"}]], " ", 
      SuperscriptBox[
       SubscriptBox["a", "j"], "\[Dagger]"]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"cb56348a-225e-d74f-ad9d-146f14549f9b"],
 ". In boson sampling, single photons enter the interferometer and the \
probability of a detection pattern is the squared permanent of a submatrix of \
W, which is believed to be hard to sample classically . The weak point is the \
source, single photons on demand are hard to make, and spontaneous parametric \
down-conversion emits them at random times. Gaussian boson sampling (GBS) \
feeds the interferometer with squeezed vacuum instead, which a nonlinear \
crystal produces deterministically, and the permanents become hafnians . Many \
photonic quantum advantage experiments  are related to GBS machines "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0021256986288376`*^9, 4.002125874117649*^9}, {
  4.002125944366823*^9, 4.0021259570797977`*^9}, {4.002126032314207*^9, 
  4.002126042133461*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"eeb1d30b-c9d7-794c-a7a7-\
64ab5e5ff9de"]
}, Open  ]],

